    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d262">

                <pb facs="0359" n="253" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg253"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00 Congress, Communist International, VII/86:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-08-27T19:00:00+03:00">August 27, 1935—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 27—1:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>394. My No. 393. Careful perusal of Krestinski’s note leads me to recommend that the measures
                    suggested in my numbers 363 to 369 of August 21<anchor n="64a" xml:id="d262-f1"
                    /> should be put into effect without delay.</p>
                <p>I venture to suggest that if the President should touch the question of the
                    connection between the Soviet Government and the Communist International he
                    should state clearly the simple fact that both the Soviet Government and the
                    Communist International are merely different aspects of Stalin’s mind and will.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
